ceae; genus:Pseudideonella; species:Pseudideonella sakaiensis\",\"synonyms\":[\"Ideonella sakaiensis\",\"Piscinibacter sakaiensis (Tanasupawat et al. 2016) Liu et al. 2023\"],\"also_known_as\":[\"Ideonella sakaiensis\",\"Piscinibacter sakaiensis\"],\"growth_temp_min\":null,\"growth_temp_max\":null,\"plastics\":[{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},{\"id\":62,\"name\":\"PEF\",\"abbreviation\":\"PEF\"},{\"id\":47,\"name\":\"PBS\",\"abbreviation\":\"PBS\"}],\"interactions\":[{\"id\":19,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"microorganism\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"Isolated by limiting dilution of PET-degrading microbial consortium no. 46. Uses PET as its major energy and carbon source; a subconsortium lacking I. sakaiensis lost PET-degradation capability, indicating the strain is functionally responsible.\",\"strain_occurrence\":{\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\",\"genome_assembly_accession\":null,\"culture_collection_ids\":{\"NBRC\":\"110686\",\"TISTR\":\"2288\"},\"sample_source\":{\"id\":3,\"country\":\"Japan\",\"region\":\"Sakai, Osaka\",\"latitude\":null,\"longitude\":null,\"environment_type\":\"industrial\",\"sample_description\":\"Sediment sample from a PET bottle recycling site; one of 250 PET debris-contaminated environmental samples (sediment, soil, wastewater, activated sludge) collected at the site.\"},\"genetic_background\":\"unknown\",\"counts_for_species\":true},\"paper_id\":3,\"paper\":{\"id\":3,\"citation\":\"Yoshida, S., Hiraga, K., Takehana, T., Taniguchi, I., Yamaji, H., Maeda, Y., Toyohara, K., Miyamoto, K., Kimura, Y., & Oda, K. (2016). A bacterium that degrades and assimilates poly(ethylene terephthalate). Science, 351(6278), 1196-1199.\",\"title\":\"A bacterium that degrades and assimilates poly(ethylene terephthalate)\",\"doi\":\"10.1126/science.aad6359\",\"year\":2016,\"authors\":\"Yoshida, S.; Hiraga, K.; Takehana, T.; Taniguchi, I.; Yamaji, H.; Maeda, Y.; Toyohara, K.; Miyamoto, K.; Kimura, Y.; Oda, K.\",\"journal\":\"Science\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":42,\"method\":\"growth_on_plastic\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":null,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":6,\"duration_unit\":\"weeks\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":false,\"culture_medium\":\"YSV (yeast extract-sodium carbonate-vitamins) medium, changed weekly\",\"aerobicity\":\"not_reported\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":1.9,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"weight_loss\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"text_description\":\"PET film almost completely degraded after 6 weeks at 30 degrees C (Fig. 1H time course); numeric time-point values are in the figure, not the text.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"growth_observed\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Strain grew using PET film as its major energy and carbon source; cells were observed both in the culture fluid and adhering to the film.\"}]},{\"id\":43,\"method\":\"sem\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":null,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":60,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":\"YSV (yeast extract-sodium carbonate-vitamins) medium\",\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"surface_morphology\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"SEM of cells grown on PET film for 60 hours showed cells connected by appendages, with shorter appendages contacting the film surface; after washing out adherent cells the film surface was extensively damaged (Fig. 1G).\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\"},{\"id\":428,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"microorganism\",\"result\":\"inconclusive\",\"notes\":\"Microbiology Resource Announcement reporting three RNA-seq data sets, not a degradation study. Cells were cultured in 702 complex medium supplemented with 1% (wt/vol) PET from a Deer Park water bottle and sampled at ~27 h 'when the cells were most likely to begin utilizing the plastic', but no degradation assay of any kind was performed in this work: no weight loss, monomer release, product identification, clear zone or surface analysis is reported, and PET was supplied alongside a complex medium rather than as the sole carbon source, so the observed growth is not attributable to PET. The abstract's statement that the organism is 'capable of metabolizing and fermenting PET' is background attributed to prior studies (refs 1-3), not a finding of this paper. Result recorded as inconclusive because this study makes no determination of its own; the PET-degrading capability of this strain is established elsewhere (e.g. Yoshida et al. 2016, Science 351:1196-1199).\",\"strain_occurrence\":{\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\",\"genome_assembly_accession\":null,\"culture_collection_ids\":{\"NBRC\":\"110686\"},\"sample_source\":null,\"genetic_background\":\"unknown\",\"counts_for_species\":true},\"paper_id\":34,\"paper\":{\"id\":34,\"citation\":\"Novak, J. K., & Gardner, J. G. (2026). Transcriptome data sets of Piscinibacter sakaiensis grown in 702 complex medium, maltose, and PET plastic. Microbiology Resource Announcements. https://doi.org/10.1128/mra.01466-25\",\"title\":\"Transcriptome data sets of <i>Piscinibacter sakaiensis<\\/i> grown in 702 complex medium, maltose, and PET plastic.\",\"doi\":\"10.1128/mra.01466-25\",\"year\":2026,\"authors\":\"Novak, J.K.; Gardner, J.G.\",\"journal\":\"Microbiology Resource Announcements\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"United States\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":637,\"method\":\"growth_on_plastic\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":null,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":27,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"whole_polymer\",\"sole_carbon_source\":false,\"culture_medium\":\"702 complex medium with 1% (wt/vol) PET plastic\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":false,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"growth_observed\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Cells were grown in 702 complex medium containing 1% (wt/vol) PET at 30 °C with high aeration and reached the target sampling point at ~27 h; growth was monitored by OD600 but no OD600 value is reported for the PET condition, and PET was not the sole carbon source, so growth cannot be attributed to the plastic.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\"},{\"id\":5645,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"microorganism\",\"result\":\"inconclusive\",\"notes\":\"This is a proteomics study, not a degradation study: no degradation of PET was measured (no weight loss, no monomer release, no clear zone, no surface analysis). PET was supplied as a 1% (w/v) amorphous cryo-milled powder on top of a yeast-extract-containing medium, so it was never a sole carbon source, and growth in the presence of PET was NOT significantly different from the no-PET control. The evidence bearing on degradation within this paper is indirect and internally mixed: secreted MHETase was the most strongly induced protein upon PET exposure (log2 = 8.6, p = 1.5E-04), while PETase - the enzyme that actually acts on the polymer - was detected but NOT differentially expressed in either the intracellular or the secreted proteome. Result is therefore recorded as inconclusive on this paper's own evidence. I. sakaiensis is an established PET degrader from prior work (Yoshida et al. 2016), but that evidence is external to this record.\",\"strain_occurrence\":{\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\",\"genome_assembly_accession\":null,\"culture_collection_ids\":null,\"sample_source\":{\"id\":1574,\"country\":null,\"region\":null,\"latitude\":null,\"longitude\":null,\"environment_type\":\"plastic_surface\",\"sample_description\":\"Not isolated in this study. The paper states only that I. sakaiensis was originally isolated from a plastisphere; the strain used here was a cryo-stock obtained from the Bioresource Collection and Research Center. No isolation locality is given in this paper.\"},\"genetic_background\":\"unknown\",\"counts_for_species\":true},\"paper_id\":271,\"paper\":{\"id\":271,\"citation\":\"Poulsen, J. S., & Nielsen, J. L. (2023). Proteomic characterisation of polyethylene terephthalate and monomer degradation by Ideonella sakaiensis. Journal of Proteomics, 279, Article 104888. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jprot.2023.104888\",\"title\":\"Proteomic characterisation of polyethylene terephthalate and monomer degradation by Ideonella sakaiensis\",\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.jprot.2023.104888\",\"year\":2023,\"authors\":\"Poulsen, J.S.; Nielsen, J.L.\",\"journal\":\"Journal of Proteomics\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Denmark\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":13630,\"method\":\"growth_on_plastic\",\"temperature\":null,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":16,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"powder\",\"sole_carbon_source\":false,\"culture_medium\":\"yeast, sulphate and vitamin (YSV) medium (0.1 g/L yeast extract, 1 g/L ammonium sulphate, 0.1 g/L calcium carbonate, trace elements and vitamin mix, in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.78,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"growth_observed\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Over 16 h of real-time monitoring, the presence of 1% (w/v) amorphous PET powder did not significantly influence the growth of I. sakaiensis compared with a control without PET (n = 8 per condition); no inhibitory and no stimulating effect was detected.\"}]},{\"id\":13631,\"method\":\"lc_ms\",\"temperature\":null,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":null,\"duration_unit\":null,\"substrate_form\":\"powder\",\"sole_carbon_source\":false,\"culture_medium\":\"yeast, sulphate and vitamin (YSV) medium in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.78,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":6,\"unit\":\"proteins\",\"text_description\":\"Of 1072 intracellular proteins detected upon exposure to PET, 6 were differentially expressed - 4 increased and 2 decreased in abundance relative to the no-PET control.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":7.2,\"unit\":\"log2 fold change\",\"text_description\":\"ATP synthase subunit c (A0A0K8P094) showed the highest increase in intracellular abundance upon PET exposure (log2 = 7.2, p = 1.3E-03).\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":2.5,\"unit\":\"log2 fold change\",\"text_description\":\"Enoyl-CoA hydratase (A0A0K8P1Z2), the second step of the beta-oxidation pathway, showed the second highest increase upon PET exposure (log2 = 2.5, p = 0.036).\"}]},{\"id\":13632,\"method\":\"lc_ms\",\"temperature\":null,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":null,\"duration_unit\":null,\"substrate_form\":\"powder\",\"sole_carbon_source\":false,\"culture_medium\":\"yeast, sulphate and vitamin (YSV) medium in 10 mM phosphate buffer pH 7\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.78,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":6,\"unit\":\"proteins\",\"text_description\":\"Of 1019 secreted proteins detected upon exposure to PET, 6 were differentially expressed - 4 increased and 2 decreased in abundance relative to the no-PET control.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":8.6,\"unit\":\"log2 fold change\",\"text_description\":\"Mono(2-hydroxyethyl) terephthalate hydrolase (MHETase, A0A0K8P8E7) was the most strongly induced secreted protein upon exposure to PET (log2 = 8.6, p = 1.5E-04) - one of the two enzymes enabling I. sakaiensis to metabolise PET.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":6.5,\"unit\":\"log2 fold change\",\"text_description\":\"4-hydroxythreonine-4-phosphate dehydrogenase (A0A0K8P8F3) increased in the secretome upon PET exposure (log2 = 6.5, p = 5.8E-04).\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6\"}],\"indirect_interactions\":[{\"id\":10,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"protein\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"Wild-type I. sakaiensis PET hydrolase (mesophilic; labile above 37 C). Baseline scaffold. Recombinantly expressed in Pseudomonas putida KT2440.\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":2,\"paper\":{\"id\":2,\"citation\":\"Lu, H., Diaz, D. J., Czarnecki, N. J., Zhu, C., Kim, W., Shroff, R., Acosta, D. J., Alexander, B. R., Cole, H. O., Zhang, Y., Lynd, N. A., Ellington, A. D., & Alper, H. S. (2022). Machine learning-aided engineering of hydrolases for PET depolymerization. Nature, 604(7907), 662-667.\",\"title\":\"Machine learning-aided engineering of hydrolases for PET depolymerization\",\"doi\":\"10.1038/s41586-022-04599-z\",\"year\":2022,\"authors\":\"Lu, H.; Diaz, D.J.; Czarnecki, N.J.; Zhu, C.; Kim, W.; Shroff, R.; Acosta, D.J.; Alexander, B.R.; Cole, H.O.; Zhang, Y.; Lynd, N.A.; Ellington, A.D.; Alper, H.S.\",\"journal\":\"Nature\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"United States\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":26,\"method\":\"hplc\",\"temperature\":50,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":8,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":24,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":1.2,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":false,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":0.231,\"unit\":\"mM\",\"text_description\":\"~0.23 mM monomers from bean cake pc-PET at 50 C, 24 h - far lower than FAST-PETase (Fig. 3b)\"}]},{\"id\":27,\"method\":\"hplc\",\"temperature\":50,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":8,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":24,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":false,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":0.57,\"unit\":\"mM\",\"text_description\":\"~0.57 mM monomers from pretreated bottle film at 50 C, 24 h (Fig. 4c)\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":\"PETase\",\"indirect_source_url\":\"/proteins/PBFCTW\",\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":null},{\"id\":20,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"protein\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"ISF6_4831 gene product, designated PETase. Shares 51% amino acid identity and catalytic residues with the Thermobifida fusca hydrolase TfH. Transcript level during growth on PET film was the highest among all analysed coding sequences (15x, 31x and 41x that on maltose, TPA-Na and BHET respectively), indicating induction by PET film itself. Extracellular; predicted localisation in supplementary text S1.\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":3,\"paper\":{\"id\":3,\"citation\":\"Yoshida, S., Hiraga, K., Takehana, T., Taniguchi, I., Yamaji, H., Maeda, Y., Toyohara, K., Miyamoto, K., Kimura, Y., & Oda, K. (2016). A bacterium that degrades and assimilates poly(ethylene terephthalate). Science, 351(6278), 1196-1199.\",\"title\":\"A bacterium that degrades and assimilates poly(ethylene terephthalate)\",\"doi\":\"10.1126/science.aad6359\",\"year\":2016,\"authors\":\"Yoshida, S.; Hiraga, K.; Takehana, T.; Taniguchi, I.; Yamaji, H.; Maeda, Y.; Toyohara, K.; Miyamoto, K.; Kimura, Y.; Oda, K.\",\"journal\":\"Science\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":44,\"method\":\"sem\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":18,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"surface_morphology\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Prominent pitting developed on the PET film surface after 18 hours with 50 nM PETase at 30 degrees C, pH 7.0.\"}]},{\"id\":45,\"method\":\"hplc\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":18,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"product_identified\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"MHET (mono(2-hydroxyethyl) terephthalic acid) was the major product released from PET film, together with minor amounts of TPA and BHET.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":\"mM\",\"text_description\":\"TPA released as a minor product of PET film hydrolysis; quantitative values are in Fig. 2B, not the text.\"}]},{\"id\":46,\"method\":\"enzyme_assay\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":18,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":120,\"unit\":\"fold vs TfH\",\"text_description\":\"PETase activity against PET film was 120 times that of TfH, 5.5 times that of LCC, and 88 times that of FsC at 30 degrees C, pH 7.0.\"},{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Catalytic preference for PET film over pNP-aliphatic esters was 380, 48 and 400 times as high as that of TfH, LCC and FsC respectively; PETase's activity on pNP-aliphatic esters was lower than all three.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":\"PETase\",\"indirect_source_url\":\"/proteins/PBFCTW\",\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":null},{\"id\":58,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"protein\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"Wild-type IsPETase is the reference enzyme throughout. It hydrolyses Gf-PET film to TPA + MHET, but poorly at 45 °C and above (Tm 47.8 °C, half-life ~4 h at 40 °C). Both the crude secretion-supernatant screen and the 72 h purified-enzyme HPLC quantification were run on it.\",\"strain_occurrence\":null,\"paper_id\":8,\"paper\":{\"id\":8,\"citation\":\"Han, Y., Xing, S., Ding, M., Wang, Y., Xiao, W., Jiang, Y., & Yao, M. (2026). Synergistic engineering PETase reveals loop-region mutations for enhanced catalytic activity and thermal stability. Synthetic and Systems Biotechnology, 11, 419-429. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.synbio.2025.10.001\",\"title\":\"Synergistic engineering PETase reveals loop-region mutations for enhanced catalytic activity and thermal stability.\",\"doi\":\"10.1016/j.synbio.2025.10.001\",\"year\":2026,\"authors\":\"Han, Y.; Xing, S.; Ding, M.; Wang, Y.; Xiao, W.; Jiang, Y.; Yao, M.\",\"journal\":\"Synthetic and Systems Biotechnology\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"China\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":138,\"method\":\"uv_vis\",\"temperature\":45,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":9,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":24,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":true,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"activity_detected\",\"value\":1.25,\"unit\":\"AU\",\"text_description\":\"Wild-type IsPETase: A254 = 1.25 after 24 h with Gf-PET film at 45 °C in the high-throughput screen (wild-type IsPETase = 1.25 on the same scale). Read from Fig. 1b.\"}]},{\"id\":139,\"method\":\"hplc\",\"temperature\":40,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":9,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":72,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":true,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":830,\"unit\":\"μM\",\"text_description\":\"Wild-type IsPETase released 830 μM total PET monomers (TPA + MHET) from Gf-PET film after 72 h at 40 °C, pH 9.0. Read from Fig. 2a; the paper's own fold-changes imply a wild-type baseline nearer 1000 μM (see extraction notes).\"}]},{\"id\":140,\"method\":\"hplc\",\"temperature\":45,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":9,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":72,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":null,\"culture_medium\":null,\"aerobicity\":null,\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":null,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":true,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"monomer_released\",\"value\":189,\"unit\":\"μM\",\"text_description\":\"Wild-type IsPETase released 189 μM total PET monomers (TPA + MHET) from Gf-PET film after 72 h at 45 °C, pH 9.0. Read from Fig. 2b.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":\"PETase\",\"indirect_source_url\":\"/proteins/PBFCTW\",\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":null}],\"construct_interactions\":[{\"id\":10157,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"microorganism\",\"result\":\"negative\",\"notes\":\"The paper names this organism 'Ideonella sakaiensis'; NCBI Taxonomy has since reclassified the species and the current accepted name for taxon 1547922 is Pseudideonella sakaiensis (Lu and Chen 2026), with Ideonella sakaiensis retained as a synonym, so the accepted name is stored here. PETase-gene disruptant, constructed in this study from the ΔpyrF parent; loss of the PETase protein was confirmed by Western blot (Fig. 4A). Tested against PET film under identical conditions to the parent and showed no degradation at all: no detectable weight change and a featureless SEM surface after 10 days, with growth on PET falling to the no-carbon-source level. The authors conclude that PETase is the sole enzyme able to hydrolyse PET in this bacterium. Graded negative — the pair was tested and no activity was observed. Growth on terephthalic acid was unimpaired (Fig. 5C), showing the defect is specific to PET and not a general growth defect; that TPA experiment yields no interaction of its own because terephthalic acid is a compound, not a plastic (R-16).\",\"strain_occurrence\":{\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6 ΔpyrF Δpetase\",\"genome_assembly_accession\":null,\"culture_collection_ids\":null,\"sample_source\":null,\"genetic_background\":\"knockout\",\"counts_for_species\":false},\"paper_id\":512,\"paper\":{\"id\":512,\"citation\":\"Hachisuka, S.-I., Nishii, T., & Yoshida, S. (2021). Development of a targeted gene disruption system in the poly(ethylene terephthalate)-degrading bacterium Ideonella sakaiensis and its applications to PETase and MHETase genes. Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 87(18), e00020-21. https://doi.org/10.1128/AEM.00020-21\",\"title\":\"Development of a Targeted Gene Disruption System in the Poly(Ethylene Terephthalate)-Degrading Bacterium Ideonella sakaiensis and Its Applications to PETase and MHETase Genes.\",\"doi\":\"10.1128/aem.00020-21\",\"year\":2021,\"authors\":\"Hachisuka, S.-I.; Nishii, T.; Yoshida, S.\",\"journal\":\"Applied and Environmental Microbiology\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":23983,\"method\":\"weight_loss\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":10,\"duration_unit\":\"days\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"weight_loss\",\"value\":0,\"unit\":\"mg\",\"text_description\":\"No weight change of the PET film was detected after 10 days. The corresponding bar in Fig. 6B (bar b) sits flat on the zero line with no visible height, against 6.9 mg for the parent strain; the Results text states no weight change was detected.\"}]},{\"id\":23984,\"method\":\"sem\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":10,\"duration_unit\":\"days\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"surface_morphology\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"No degradation traces: the PET film surface is smooth and essentially featureless, with none of the pitting or ridge network seen for the parent strain (Fig. 6Ab, scale bar 1 µm). Qualitative image — no number to read.\"}]},{\"id\":23985,\"method\":\"growth_on_plastic\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":240,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"growth_observed\",\"value\":0.15,\"unit\":\"OD660\",\"text_description\":\"Essentially no growth attributable to PET: OD660 reached only about 0.15 by 240 h, and was about 0.10 at 144 h against about 0.78 for the parent strain at the same time point — approximately 7-fold lower, as stated in the Results. The trace matches the strain's own no-carbon-source control (Fig. 5A, plateau about 0.11-0.12). Time course read from Fig. 5B, triangles: approximately 0.06 (48 h), 0.06 (72 h), 0.10 (96 h), 0.10 (120 h), 0.10 (144 h), 0.15 (192 h), 0.15 (240 h). Values read off the plot; precision about ±0.02.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6 ΔpyrF Δpetase\"},{\"id\":10158,\"plastic\":{\"id\":16,\"name\":\"PET\",\"abbreviation\":\"PET\"},\"actor_type\":\"microorganism\",\"result\":\"positive\",\"notes\":\"The paper names this organism 'Ideonella sakaiensis'; NCBI Taxonomy has since reclassified the species and the current accepted name for taxon 1547922 is Pseudideonella sakaiensis (Lu and Chen 2026), with Ideonella sakaiensis retained as a synonym, so the accepted name is stored here. MHETase-gene disruptant, constructed in this study from the ΔpyrF parent; loss of the MHETase protein was confirmed by Western blot (Fig. 4B). It still degraded the PET film — 1.4 mg weight loss over 10 days, approximately 20% of the parent's 6.9 mg, with partial surface defects visible by SEM — while its growth on PET fell to the no-carbon-source level. The authors' determination is explicit: 'the Δmhetase strain degraded PET', and 'MHETase is not essential for PET degradation but important for assimilating it'. Graded positive on that determination together with the SEM surface defects. The 1.4 mg loss is roughly 1.6% of the ca. 85 mg film, below the 2% mass-loss threshold; the threshold does not decide this pair, because the authors state a determination of their own and the mass loss is not the only evidence. As with the other two strains, growth on terephthalic acid was unimpaired (Fig. 5C) and yields no interaction — terephthalic acid is a compound, not a plastic (R-16).\",\"strain_occurrence\":{\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6 ΔpyrF Δmhetase\",\"genome_assembly_accession\":null,\"culture_collection_ids\":null,\"sample_source\":null,\"genetic_background\":\"knockout\",\"counts_for_species\":false},\"paper_id\":512,\"paper\":{\"id\":512,\"citation\":\"Hachisuka, S.-I., Nishii, T., & Yoshida, S. (2021). Development of a targeted gene disruption system in the poly(ethylene terephthalate)-degrading bacterium Ideonella sakaiensis and its applications to PETase and MHETase genes. Applied and Environmental Microbiology, 87(18), e00020-21. https://doi.org/10.1128/AEM.00020-21\",\"title\":\"Development of a Targeted Gene Disruption System in the Poly(Ethylene Terephthalate)-Degrading Bacterium Ideonella sakaiensis and Its Applications to PETase and MHETase Genes.\",\"doi\":\"10.1128/aem.00020-21\",\"year\":2021,\"authors\":\"Hachisuka, S.-I.; Nishii, T.; Yoshida, S.\",\"journal\":\"Applied and Environmental Microbiology\",\"corresponding_author_country\":\"Japan\"},\"experiments\":[{\"id\":23986,\"method\":\"weight_loss\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":10,\"duration_unit\":\"days\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"weight_loss\",\"value\":1.4,\"unit\":\"mg\",\"text_description\":\"1.4 mg of PET film lost after 10 days of cultivation at 30 °C (mean of three independent experiments), approximately 20% of the 6.9 mg lost by the parent strain. Read from the bar chart in Fig. 6B, bar c, and stated as 1.4 mg in the Results.\"}]},{\"id\":23987,\"method\":\"sem\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":10,\"duration_unit\":\"days\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"surface_morphology\",\"value\":null,\"unit\":null,\"text_description\":\"Partial surface defects: eroded, ridged patches cover part of the field while the remainder of the film is smooth — intermediate between the parent strain's uniformly pitted surface and the featureless Δpetase surface (Fig. 6Ac, scale bar 1 µm). Qualitative image — no number to read.\"}]},{\"id\":23988,\"method\":\"growth_on_plastic\",\"temperature\":30,\"temperature_unit\":\"C\",\"ph\":7.4,\"buffer\":null,\"duration\":240,\"duration_unit\":\"hours\",\"substrate_form\":\"amorphous_film\",\"sole_carbon_source\":true,\"culture_medium\":\"sodium carbonate-vitamin (SV) minimal medium with uracil (10 µg/ml) (SV-Ura)\",\"aerobicity\":\"aerobic\",\"substrate_crystallinity_pct\":2.4,\"substrate_mw\":null,\"substrate_mw_unit\":null,\"analytical_grade\":null,\"assay_result\":null,\"assay_result_notes\":null,\"detection_limit\":null,\"detection_limit_unit\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling\":null,\"quantitation_ceiling_unit\":null,\"initial_mass\":null,\"initial_mass_unit\":null,\"observations\":[{\"time_point\":null,\"time_point_unit\":null,\"sd\":null,\"n\":null,\"control_value\":null,\"measurement_type\":\"growth_observed\",\"value\":0.19,\"unit\":\"OD660\",\"text_description\":\"Growth on PET reached only about 0.19 OD660 by 240 h, and was about 0.11 at 144 h against about 0.78 for the parent strain — approximately 7-fold lower, as stated in the Results — matching the strain's own no-carbon-source control (Fig. 5A, plateau about 0.11-0.12). Time course read from Fig. 5B, squares: approximately 0.07 (48 h), 0.06 (72 h), 0.10 (96 h), 0.12 (120 h), 0.11 (144 h), 0.17 (192 h), 0.19 (240 h). Values read off the plot; precision about ±0.02.\"}]}],\"indirect_source_label\":null,\"indirect_source_url\":null,\"expression_hosts\":[],\"strain_designation\":\"201-F6 ΔpyrF Δmhetase\"}],\"proteins\":[{\"activity_class\":\"untested\",\"id\":3,\"kind\":\"wild_type\",\"variant_type\":null,\"parent_accession\":null,\"plasticdb_id\":\"00003\",\"accession\":\"PBFCTW\",\"name\":\"PETase\",\"genbank_id\":\"GAP38373.1\",\"has_signal_peptide\":true,\"uniprot_accession\":null,\"pdb_id\":null,\"ec_number\":null,\"source_microorganism\":null,\"degradation_links\":[],\"paper\":null,\"papers\":[],\"year\":2016,\"best_kcat_km\":null,\"best_kcat_km_unit\":null,\"updated_at\":null},{\